WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.481 --> 00:00:03.189
- Welcome to the National
Fish and Wildlife

00:00:03.189 --> 00:00:05.590
Forensics Laboratory in Ashland, Oregon.

00:00:05.590 --> 00:00:08.374
Our laboratory works very
much like a police crime lab,

00:00:08.374 --> 00:00:11.372
in that we identify evidence and we try to

00:00:11.372 --> 00:00:14.672
link suspect, victim, crime scene
together with that evidence.

00:00:14.672 --> 00:00:18.839
The big difference is that our
victim is a non-human animal.

00:00:26.003 --> 00:00:28.783
I'm Ken Goddard and the
directory of this laboratory.

00:00:28.783 --> 00:00:31.362
When our agents seize evidence,

00:00:31.362 --> 00:00:33.786
at a border, a custom center, wherever,

00:00:33.786 --> 00:00:36.484
that evidence will be
packaged and shipped to us.

00:00:36.484 --> 00:00:39.007
Our job is to analyze evidence,
collect it for wildlife

00:00:39.007 --> 00:00:41.066
law enforcement investigations.

00:00:41.066 --> 00:00:45.108
Our laboratory primarily supports
wildlife laws in general,

00:00:45.108 --> 00:00:47.668
specifically the endangered species act.

00:00:47.668 --> 00:00:49.828
Sadly, we're the only full service crime

00:00:49.828 --> 00:00:52.351
lab for wildlife in the world.

00:00:52.351 --> 00:00:55.070
We would like not to be,
but that's our reality.

00:00:55.070 --> 00:00:57.188
The illness of wildlife trade is huge,

00:00:57.188 --> 00:00:59.028
it's probably in the neighborhood of

00:00:59.028 --> 00:01:02.111
17, 18, 19 billion dollars a year.

00:01:02.111 --> 00:01:03.908
We're certainly impacting the trade,

00:01:03.908 --> 00:01:07.680
but it's a desperately, difficult job.

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:09.327
Here are some of the scientists that

00:01:09.327 --> 00:01:11.279
help us solve crimes against animals.

00:01:11.279 --> 00:01:14.893
Our veterinary pathologist
is Dr. Becky Kagan.

00:01:14.893 --> 00:01:18.275
- My job is to autopsy all of the animals

00:01:18.275 --> 00:01:20.858
that come in here and try
to figure out why they died.

00:01:20.858 --> 00:01:23.634
Today I have two birds:
they're both bald eagles;

00:01:23.634 --> 00:01:25.759
they're wild animals that are found dead.

00:01:25.759 --> 00:01:28.879
My job is to figure out,
was there a crime involved.

00:01:28.879 --> 00:01:31.375
We can do CT scanning here at the lab,

00:01:31.375 --> 00:01:34.474
we take photographs, we do
alternate life source exam

00:01:34.474 --> 00:01:37.476
to help us solve the puzzle
of why this animal died.

00:01:37.476 --> 00:01:40.754
- One aspect of our work
that's typical of a crime lab

00:01:40.754 --> 00:01:43.274
is we compare a known against an unknown.

00:01:43.274 --> 00:01:45.015
For us to identify, let's say a feather

00:01:45.015 --> 00:01:47.154
or maybe to identify the bones.

00:01:47.154 --> 00:01:49.622
We would compare that against
tissue from a known animal

00:01:49.622 --> 00:01:51.652
or to a skeleton and
the fellow responsible

00:01:51.652 --> 00:01:54.292
for our huge collection,
is Johnnie French.

00:01:54.292 --> 00:01:59.187
- Our collection is 35,000
specimens, give or take,

00:01:59.187 --> 00:02:01.085
everything that we have here,

00:02:01.085 --> 00:02:04.671
is either been donated
or was seized in customs.

00:02:04.671 --> 00:02:07.530
The uniqueness of our collection helps us,

00:02:07.530 --> 00:02:11.697
kinda keep abreast of what
we see in the wildlife trade.

00:02:12.768 --> 00:02:16.372
Most of the time, we have
to remove meat from bone,

00:02:16.372 --> 00:02:18.593
in order to examine specimens or to

00:02:18.593 --> 00:02:21.210
get them into our reference collection.

00:02:21.210 --> 00:02:24.170
Easiest way to do that
is, take the carcass

00:02:24.170 --> 00:02:26.909
and chuck it in the
flesh eating beetle tank.

00:02:26.909 --> 00:02:30.270
After the beetles are
finished with the carcass

00:02:30.270 --> 00:02:31.970
that we threw in there, we'll
wash them a couple of times

00:02:31.970 --> 00:02:35.709
and then the bones are
ready for the collection.

00:02:35.709 --> 00:02:39.088
- [Ken] Heading up our genetics
section is Dr. Mary Curtis.

00:02:39.088 --> 00:02:41.250
- The genetics team is important to the

00:02:41.250 --> 00:02:44.471
functioning of the lab
because we look at items

00:02:44.471 --> 00:02:48.050
that other parts of
the lab can't identify.

00:02:48.050 --> 00:02:50.528
Items that might be crafted to the point

00:02:50.528 --> 00:02:52.749
where you can't tell what their origin is.

00:02:52.749 --> 00:02:56.167
So we have access to over
40,000 individual tissue samples

00:02:56.167 --> 00:02:57.866
in our reference collection that

00:02:57.866 --> 00:03:00.949
we use to identify different species.

00:03:02.845 --> 00:03:05.042
- One of the more
interesting people in our lab

00:03:05.042 --> 00:03:07.906
is Dr. Pepper Trail, our ornithologist.

00:03:07.906 --> 00:03:10.103
- I'm an ornithologist, a bird expert,

00:03:10.103 --> 00:03:11.972
so I look at bird evidence and use

00:03:11.972 --> 00:03:15.024
reference specimens to
compare to evidence items

00:03:15.024 --> 00:03:17.287
to confirm the identifications.

00:03:17.287 --> 00:03:20.125
The kinds of evidence that we
receive is incredibly variant.

00:03:20.125 --> 00:03:23.266
We receive everything from
complete dead carcasses

00:03:23.266 --> 00:03:26.077
in good condition, to just bags of bones.

00:03:26.077 --> 00:03:30.456
I've identified over 750
species in the course of my work

00:03:30.456 --> 00:03:32.245
here, which would be a pretty good

00:03:32.245 --> 00:03:34.270
life list for a lot of birders.

00:03:34.270 --> 00:03:36.777
The magnitude of the international

00:03:36.777 --> 00:03:39.393
wildlife trade is staggering.

00:03:39.393 --> 00:03:42.013
Everyday, into our laboratory comes

00:03:42.013 --> 00:03:45.293
remains of dead animals
from around the world

00:03:45.293 --> 00:03:48.171
and someone needs to provide
the information needed

00:03:48.171 --> 00:03:51.395
to bring wildlife criminals to justice.

00:03:51.395 --> 00:03:53.651
It's sometimes a sad job, but it's

00:03:53.651 --> 00:03:56.952
a very important job, and I'm
proud to be a part of it.

00:03:56.952 --> 00:03:59.785
(inspiring music)

